# Jaguar 420, 1966-1968.
#
# The 420 is the S-Type body with the Mk X's front end and the 4.2-litre engine,
# built from 1966 to 1968 as the last of the compact Jaguar saloons. It is not a new
# shell: the doors, cabin, roof and tail are the S-Type's, and only the front
# differs, with four round headlamps in two pairs and the large chrome grille with
# vertical bars and a central divider. The Daimler Sovereign of 1966-69 is the same
# car with Daimler badging and is not modelled separately.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jaguar_420
#
# Dimensions 4.762 x 1.702 x 1.429 m, wheelbase 2.727 m; the published 420
# dimension box. Against the S-Type that is 12 mm longer, 19 mm wider, 13 mm taller
# and 3 mm shorter in the wheelbase.
#
# Colours: British Racing Green, Carmen Red, Old English White and Opalescent
# Silver Grey, the same Jaguar range as the S-Type. Weights follow the register's
# treatment of low-volume prestige cars rather than a measured colour split: green
# 2, red 1, white 1, silver 1. Total 5, below the S-Type's 8 because the 420 ran for
# only two years. Provisional pending the central rebase.
#
# Speed 190 km/h, as the S-Type: the 4.2 had more torque but the same road-speed
# ceiling in pakset terms. Comfort 90/465, on the line 330 + 1.5 x 90, inherited
# from the shared body. Classes 1,2,3.
#
# Dates are road presence, not production: August 1966 intro, retires staggered
# 1980-1987 on a fourteen- to twenty-one-year tail.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/jaguar-420.blend. Interchange:
# citycars/4.5/jaguar-420.dae (297 objects, the count inflated by the wire wheels).
# Variant of the Jaguar S-Type master; references:
# citycars/references/jaguar-420/ (gitignored; Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0).
#
# Accepted low-poly deviations, recorded rather than corrected: the inherited cabin
# and tail are substantially more angular than the photographed compound curves, as
# on the S-Type, and the wire wheels are modelled rather than the disc-hubcap
# alternative.

obj=citycar
